        //                        88   YD  `Y88P'   `Y88P'  YP  YP  YP      VP   V8P YP   YP YP  YP  YP Y88888P `8888Y' 
        
        // Most of the level names provide hints on how to solve the stage.
        // If you have any doubts, please check the game or contact me.




        Room0: "1 - sul mondo della luna",                // As it is a pun, there is more freedom in choosing a name for this stage.        
        Room1: "2 - salta e cambia",                // Keep the name of this stage as faithful as possible, as it serves as a kind of tutorial for the player.
        Room2: "3 - assurdo",                                // "Absurd" here is in the philosophical sense of the word, derived from absurdism.
        Room3: "4 - foglie di cemento",
        Room4: "5 - foglie... di foglie",                        // here happens a conversation with the previous stage name (which has leaves made with concrete).
        Room5: "6 - portale",                                // It has this name because the top and "floor" of the game screen serve as portals, which is a hint.
        Room6: "7 - sopra le lumache",                // It refers to a Brazilian song, so it is a pun, and you have more freedom in choosing a name that fits the context.
        Room7: "8 - non ci sono nemici",                        // It has this name because the game has no enemies, and all creatures in the game also help the player.
        Room8: "9 - il giardino delle chiocciole", 
        Room9: "10 - è vivo!",                        // In this stage, an object that is usually still starts moving around, and the name comes from Frankenstein himself.
        Room10: "11 - testa fra le nuvole",        // As it is a pun, there is more freedom in choosing a name for this stage.
        Room11: "12 - senza capo né coda",// This stage has neither a floor nor a ceiling, so it is a pun, so feel free to choose another name that fits the context.
        Room12: "13 - fauna aerea",
        Room13: "14 - va e viene",
        Room14: "15 - fragile",
        Room15: "16 - supporto",                                // A creature in the game serves as a support for the player's feet in this stage, and the support here is in a physical sense.
        Room16: "17 - insetto sotto il tetto",                        // The name does not provide any hints, but I chose it because it sounds good. in Portuguese it is called "inseto no teto" (bugs on the celling)
        Room17: "18 - guardando le stelle",                // It is a pun, and to solve this stage, the player needs to headbutt a block.
        Room18: "19 - medusa",                                // It has these names because this stage has creatures made of stone.
        Room19: "20 - scala per insetti",
        Room20: "21 - cimitero dei fiori",
        Room21: "22 - la vita è fatta di cambiamenti", // It refers to the poem.
        Room22: "23 - malinteso",
        Room23: "24 - gira gira",                        // The original (vira-vira) is a pun that only works on portuguese, there is more freedom in choosing a name for this stage.        
        Room24: "25 - sotto le spine",
        Room25: "26 - tornare alla normalità",
        Room26: "27 - al primo colpo",                        // To complete this stage, at the beginning of it, the player already needs to know what they are doing and needs to pass it on the first try.
        Room27: "28 - $jumpscare$",
        Room28: "29 - dammi dammi",        //referring to the song. I add several references and jokes to make everything humorous.
        Room29: "30 - probabilmente impossibile",
        Room30: "31 - polvere di stelle",
        Room31: "32 - di fianco",                        //the name is humorous in Portuguese, there is more freedom in choosing a name for this stage
        Room32: "33 - rivelazione?",
        Room33: "34 - tempo inesorabile",
        Room34: "35 - danza delle ombre",
        Room35: "36 - senza controllo",
        Room36: "37 - senza spazio",
        Room37: "38 - senza tempo",                        // I would love for these three stages to continue with the same structure, rhyming with each other.
    Room38: "39 - trampolino",
